    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. Let's dive deeper into those contributors while maintaining the academic structure and integrity of the draft:\n\n---\n\nSocial Capital Theory has gone through an intricate scholarly evolution, delving into the multifaceted ways in which social networks and relationships create benefits at both individual and collective levels. Pierre Bourdieu significantly expanded the theory's horizons with his concept of \"cultural capital\" (1986), suggesting that social and cultural assets can impact individuals' life chances. James Coleman's instrumental analysis (1988) of social capital illuminated how social activities such as school clubs or sports teams are pivotal in equipping individuals with education, thereby impacting academic success. Robert Putnam (2000) played a key role in popularizing Social Capital on a grand scale; his distinction between \"bonding\" (homogenous group) and \"bridging\" (heterogeneous group) social capital set the stage for understanding their distinct functions. Mark Granovetter's (1985) pioneering work on the strength of weak ties provided a compelling argument that loose, infrequent interactions carry significant potential for resource acquisition and information flow.\n\nSubsequent scholars have further nuanced these foundational theories. Lin (2001, 2005) offered valuable insights into trust as a core component of social capital,",
      "source": "target",
